.TH OSC.*.SLOW_READ_PCT 4 2026-11-24 Lustre "Lustre Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
osc.slow_read_pct \- percent of usual read latency before trying another mirror
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY
.BI "lctl get_param osc." INSTANCE .slow_read_pct
.SY
.BI "lctl set_param osc." INSTANCE .slow_read_pct= PERCENT
.SY
.BI "lctl set_param osc." INSTANCE .slow_read_min_ms= MSEC
.SY
.BI "lctl get_param osc." INSTANCE .slow_read_stats
.SY
.BI "lctl set_param osc." INSTANCE .slow_read_stats= 0
.YS
.SS PROPERTIES
.TP
.B Access Permissions
.br
.BR 644 " | " -rw-r--r--
.TP
.B Scope
.br
Per OSC device
.TP
.B Config
.br
Always present
.TP
.B Default
.br
.RB slow_read_pct= 300
.br
.RB slow_read_min_ms= 0
(250 for a non-rotational OST, 2000 otherwise, or until the client has
fetched the OST's statfs data, e.g. with
.BR "lfs df" )
.TP
.B Valid Range
.br
.RB slow_read_pct= 0
(off) or any percentage of the smoothed read latency
.br
.RB slow_read_min_ms= 0
(default for the OST) or any number of milliseconds
.SH DESCRIPTION
A read of a mirrored or erasure-coded (EC) file normally waits for an
OST until the RPC's adaptive timeout expires, which can be many seconds,
or up to at_max seconds while a slow OST keeps sending early replies.
.B slow_read_pct
bounds that wait for files that have another source for the data.
.PP
Each OSC keeps a smoothed latency and its mean deviation for each read
RPC size, as TCP does for its retransmission timer.
A read RPC of an EC or mirrored file gets a soft deadline, counted from
when it is sent, of the largest of
.I PERCENT
percent of the smoothed latency, the smoothed latency plus four times its
deviation, and
.BR slow_read_min_ms .
With no estimate for the RPC size yet, the estimate for the nearest
smaller size is scaled up by the size ratio; with none at all, only
.B slow_read_min_ms
applies.
Past the deadline the RPC is abandoned without the import being failed,
and the client reads the data from another mirror, or reconstructs it
from the parity of an EC file.
.PP
After an abandoned read the OSC treats the OST as slow for four times
that soft deadline, or until a read on it succeeds, and EC reads avoid it
meanwhile.
Each further abandoned read before a successful one doubles both the
deadline and the time the OST is avoided, up to the adaptive timeout, so
an OST that stays slow is probed less and less often.
An OST that is down is disconnected by the client, and reads are routed
around it at once whatever the value of this parameter.
.PP
Reconstructing part of an EC raid set reads all of its other data
stripes, many times the size of the read.
A read that touches only part of a raid set therefore gets no soft
deadline, and is not reconstructed around a slow OST, when most of the
raid set's other stripes are on loaded OSTs, since the extra reads would
add to that load.
An OST counts as loaded when its smoothed latency for the read size is
above a limit of two to four times its median latency since mount, and
above an eighth of
.BR slow_read_min_ms .
That limit is twice the upper bound of the power-of-two
.B osc.*.io_latency_stats
bucket that holds the median.
.PP
Reads issued by a reconstruction itself are never abandoned, and neither
are reads whose error could not be retried: an asynchronous direct read,
or a direct read of a plain mirrored file, including a buffered read that
hybrid I/O makes direct.
A slow OST is skipped only when the parity can stand in for every
unavailable stripe of its raid set; otherwise the read waits for it.
A read that no in-sync parity covers is served like a plain mirror's, and
with no other mirror it waits.
.PP
The OST is not told that a read was abandoned.
It still reads the data and sends it, and the client discards it, so the
transfer does not hold an OST service thread.
Only when the client cannot set up a buffer to discard the data into,
because too many abandoned reads are outstanding or memory is short, does
the transfer hold a service thread until it times out and log a bulk read
error.
.PP
.B slow_read_stats
shows the parameters, how many milliseconds the OST is still avoided,
the current backoff, how many reads were abandoned since the counters
were last cleared, and the smoothed latency and deviation in microseconds
per RPC size.
Writing to it clears the estimates, the backoff, the mark and the
counter.
.SH EXAMPLES
Give up on reads only after five times their usual latency, then check
one OST:
.EX
.RB client# " lctl set_param osc.*.slow_read_pct=500"
.RB client# " lctl get_param osc.testfs-OST0003-osc-*.slow_read_stats"
osc.testfs-OST0003-osc-ffff8803c9c0f000.slow_read_stats=
slow_read_pct: 500
slow_read_min_ms: 2000
slow_until_ms: 0
backoff: 0
soft_timeouts: 1
read_latency_us:
  4K: { average: 792, deviation: 210 }
  1024K: { average: 7103, deviation: 1802 }
.EE
.PP
Wait at least one second for any read from SSD OSTs:
.EX
.RB client# " lctl set_param osc.*.slow_read_min_ms=1000"
.EE
.SH AVAILABILITY
.B osc.*.slow_read_pct
is part of the
.BR lustre (7)
filesystem package since release 2.18.0.
.\" Added in commit 2.17.5x
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lfs-setstripe (1),
.BR at_min (4),
.BR lctl-set_param (8)
